WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:02.450
SIERRA: Hi everyone.

00:00:02.450 --> 00:00:07.850
It’s been more than two months since the
war in Ukraine began.

00:00:07.850 --> 00:00:11.920
At first, many people assumed that the conflict
wouldn’t happen.

00:00:11.920 --> 00:00:15.799
Then, many assumed that it would end with
a quick Russian victory.

00:00:15.799 --> 00:00:21.250
Now, after incredible bravery by Ukrainian
armed forces, we know that we are looking

00:00:21.250 --> 00:00:23.720
at a prolonged conflict.

00:00:23.720 --> 00:00:25.830
Ukraine has pushed back hard.

00:00:25.830 --> 00:00:30.199
Russia seems to have given up on Kyiv, at
least for the time being, and is preparing

00:00:30.199 --> 00:00:34.820
for a renewed assault on the Eastern and Southern
regions of Ukraine.

00:00:34.820 --> 00:00:40.180
Meanwhile, more than 11 million Ukrainians
are believed to have fled their homes, with

00:00:40.180 --> 00:00:43.079
more than 5 million fleeing the country.

00:00:43.079 --> 00:00:45.220
Casualties continue to rise.

00:00:45.220 --> 00:00:50.460
And as focus on NATO grows, we still don’t
know whether this regional war could turn

00:00:50.460 --> 00:00:52.590
into a war between superpowers.

00:00:52.590 --> 00:00:58.470
With all of that in mind, we thought it was
time to sit back down with CFR president Richard

00:00:58.470 --> 00:00:59.470
Haass.

00:00:59.470 --> 00:01:03.910
We talked last week, and a lot has happened
since then including an official visit to

00:01:03.910 --> 00:01:09.610
Ukraine by Secretary of State Tony Blinken
and Secretary of Defense Lloyd Austin.

00:01:09.610 --> 00:01:14.060
But I learned a lot in the conversation, and
I got to pick Richard’s brain about what

00:01:14.060 --> 00:01:16.540
this war means, and where it's headed.

00:01:16.540 --> 00:01:18.630
And we wanted to share that with our listeners.

00:01:18.630 --> 00:01:22.220
I’m Gabrielle Sierra and this is Why It
Matters.

00:01:22.220 --> 00:01:29.620
Today, the path forward in Ukraine, with Dr.
Richard Haass.

00:01:29.620 --> 00:01:31.170
SIERRA: Here we are again.

00:01:31.170 --> 00:01:32.200
Richard HAASS: Good to be back.

00:01:32.200 --> 00:01:34.579
SIERRA: Welcome back to Why It Matters, Richard
Haass.

00:01:34.579 --> 00:01:36.990
Again, wish it was under better circumstances.

00:01:36.990 --> 00:01:37.990
But -

00:01:37.990 --> 00:01:40.619
HAASS: You wouldn't have me back if the circumstances
were all good.

00:01:40.619 --> 00:01:41.619
SIERRA: That's a good point, actually.

00:01:41.619 --> 00:01:42.619
Yeah.

00:01:42.619 --> 00:01:43.619
That's true.

00:01:43.619 --> 00:01:47.719
Ok let’s get right into it, so you have
a new piece out in Foreign Affairs, and in

00:01:47.719 --> 00:01:54.070
it, you note that while Russia's goals in
this war seem pretty clear, the goals of the

00:01:54.070 --> 00:01:56.020
West aren't very clear.

00:01:56.020 --> 00:02:03.460
So what should our goals be and what should
we be unwilling to accept in terms of how

00:02:03.460 --> 00:02:04.630
this war ends?

00:02:04.630 --> 00:02:08.830
HAASS: Russia's goals, as you say, seem, and
I probably emphasize the word “seem” pretty

00:02:08.830 --> 00:02:09.830
clear.

00:02:09.830 --> 00:02:10.830
SIERRA: Yeah.

00:02:10.830 --> 00:02:13.280
HAASS: You never know what's real and what's
put out, but the goals to begin with were

00:02:13.280 --> 00:02:18.330
quite expansive, obviously was all of Ukraine
to effectively end its sovereignty, probably

00:02:18.330 --> 00:02:22.110
establish a puppet government, that didn't
work out so well for Mr. Putin.

00:02:22.110 --> 00:02:24.660
They seemed to have downsized their goals
significantly.

00:02:24.660 --> 00:02:30.890
And now you see a lot of stuff about the Donbas,
a land corridor connecting Southern and Eastern

00:02:30.890 --> 00:02:33.019
Ukraine to Crimea.

00:02:33.019 --> 00:02:34.920
So that seems to be the current set of goals.

00:02:34.920 --> 00:02:38.819
If they ever were to achieve them, none of
us can say with confidence, they wouldn't,

00:02:38.819 --> 00:02:40.310
once again, revise their goals up.

00:02:40.310 --> 00:02:41.310
SIERRA: Right.

00:02:41.310 --> 00:02:42.430
HAASS: But I am struck by the contrast.

00:02:42.430 --> 00:02:44.209
I don't know what our goals are.

00:02:44.209 --> 00:02:47.600
And I've been watching this pretty closely,
you know, almost all the debate out there,

00:02:47.600 --> 00:02:50.330
think about it - is, are we providing enough
arms?

00:02:50.330 --> 00:02:52.760
Are we providing the right kind of arms?

00:02:52.760 --> 00:02:55.620
Are we doing everything we can and should
be in the way of sanctions?

00:02:55.620 --> 00:03:00.080
Are we doing everything we can and should
do in the way of bolstering NATO?

00:03:00.080 --> 00:03:04.060
Almost all the debate that I've heard and
read has been about means.

00:03:04.060 --> 00:03:05.060
It's not been about ends.

00:03:05.060 --> 00:03:06.810
So what's our definition of success?

00:03:06.810 --> 00:03:10.800
Is our definition of success, for example,
to simply end the war?

00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:15.020
Is it to get all Russian forces out of all
of Ukraine?

00:03:15.020 --> 00:03:19.849
Including what they gained in 2014, Crimea
and parts of the Donbas?

00:03:19.849 --> 00:03:24.879
Is it to get back to where things were two
months ago, when this phase of the war began?

00:03:24.879 --> 00:03:26.269
Is it to get a peace treaty?

00:03:26.269 --> 00:03:28.510
Is it to get accountability on war crimes?

00:03:28.510 --> 00:03:30.260
Is it to get payment for reparations?

00:03:30.260 --> 00:03:35.939
Is it to get Vladimir Putin out of power,
when the president mused about regime change?

00:03:35.939 --> 00:03:42.349
So here we are two months into the war and
I don’t think we have a shared definition

00:03:42.349 --> 00:03:45.980
of success.[a] And that's really important
because in a couple of weeks, we're probably

00:03:45.980 --> 00:03:50.560
going to be in the middle of an intense battle
in the south and the east.

00:03:50.560 --> 00:03:52.500
And what should it inform our policy?

00:03:52.500 --> 00:03:56.959
For some would say so long as Russia doesn't
get more than they now have, that ought to

00:03:56.959 --> 00:03:58.760
be something we, and the Ukrainians can live
with.

00:03:58.760 --> 00:04:01.989
Some will say that gives Russia way too much
of a reward.

00:04:01.989 --> 00:04:05.959
And that what we ought to do is get back to
where we were in late February.

00:04:05.959 --> 00:04:11.099
Some will say, there should not be one Russian
soldier on any square inch of Ukrainian territory.

00:04:11.099 --> 00:04:15.200
And a lot of people have raised their war
aims because of the atrocities.

00:04:15.200 --> 00:04:16.729
War has a way of hardening people.

00:04:16.729 --> 00:04:22.260
And the feeling is Russia has to pay a cost
for the awful things that they've done.

00:04:22.260 --> 00:04:26.380
So again, the question I have is what are
our war aims?

00:04:26.380 --> 00:04:27.990
Who sets them?

00:04:27.990 --> 00:04:31.270
Ukraine might have a different set of war
aims than the United States, which might have

00:04:31.270 --> 00:04:34.970
a different set of war aims, say than Germany
or the EU or NATO.

00:04:34.970 --> 00:04:36.900
And there's a lot of history here.

00:04:36.900 --> 00:04:41.900
In 1950, when we fought a war in Korea and
originally the North Koreans did very well,

00:04:41.900 --> 00:04:47.720
they conquered most of the peninsula, then
General McArthur landed US and UN forces at

00:04:47.720 --> 00:04:51.460
this port called Incheon marched up to the
38th parallel.

00:04:51.460 --> 00:04:54.010
And at that point, things were where they
had begun.

00:04:54.010 --> 00:04:58.139
And he and President Truman made the fateful
decision, "Wow, we're on such a role.

00:04:58.139 --> 00:04:59.250
Let's continue.

00:04:59.250 --> 00:05:04.870
Let's try to unify the entire peninsula under
the pro Western government in Seoul."

00:05:04.870 --> 00:05:09.020
And lots of people said, "Be careful because
as you go up toward the Chinese border, the

00:05:09.020 --> 00:05:10.380
Chinese won't let that happen."

00:05:10.380 --> 00:05:15.691
Hundreds of thousands of Chinese troops entered
the war, tens of thousands Americans, hundreds

00:05:15.691 --> 00:05:17.550
of thousands of Koreans died after that.

00:05:17.550 --> 00:05:21.910
And after several more years, things were
once again, pretty much stopped along the

00:05:21.910 --> 00:05:23.389
38th parallel.

00:05:23.389 --> 00:05:28.280
And I raised that because I think we need
to have a serious conversation about a couple

00:05:28.280 --> 00:05:29.280
of possibilities.

00:05:29.280 --> 00:05:31.170
One is that things go badly on the ground.

00:05:31.170 --> 00:05:35.490
Just say, Russia continues to expand some
of its holdings in the Donbas, moves towards

00:05:35.490 --> 00:05:38.590
Odessa and the rest, what more might we do
there?

00:05:38.590 --> 00:05:41.510
What do we do to prevent that or reverse it?

00:05:41.510 --> 00:05:43.131
What happens if Ukraine starts gaining the
upper hand?

00:05:43.131 --> 00:05:46.970
In the first phase of the war, Ukraine did
extraordinarily well, just say they started

00:05:46.970 --> 00:05:50.530
pushing Russian troops out of the Donbas into
Russia.

00:05:50.530 --> 00:05:53.069
And Mr. Putin then starts to threaten escalation.

00:05:53.069 --> 00:05:54.080
What do we do then?

00:05:54.080 --> 00:05:58.140
Again, what do we insist on if there are talks
that begin about a cease fire?

00:05:58.140 --> 00:05:59.889
What are our terms and so on?

00:05:59.889 --> 00:06:03.130
It's important to have some good answers to
these questions now.

00:06:03.130 --> 00:06:04.130
SIERRA: Yeah.

00:06:04.130 --> 00:06:05.130
It seems like.

00:06:05.130 --> 00:06:06.910
HAASS: The heated battle is not the best time
to start improvising.

00:06:06.910 --> 00:06:10.949
By and large, you get in trouble when you
improvise.

00:06:10.949 --> 00:06:14.250
History has a lot of examples of that, including
something I was involved with, with the Gulf

00:06:14.250 --> 00:06:15.250
war.

00:06:15.250 --> 00:06:20.599
So it's important to think this through and
my guess is there won't be consensus.

00:06:20.599 --> 00:06:22.620
There probably won't be consensus in Ukraine.

00:06:22.620 --> 00:06:25.949
There probably won't be consensus in the United
States in the administration.

00:06:25.949 --> 00:06:28.860
There won't be consensus within Europe and
NATO.

00:06:28.860 --> 00:06:33.220
And I don't want that to become something
that Mr. Putin exploits.

00:06:33.220 --> 00:06:40.500
And so I think we need to begin talking about
these to be honest, difficult issues, about

00:06:40.500 --> 00:06:42.560
who decides and what should be decided.

00:06:42.560 --> 00:06:43.860
SIERRA: Right.

00:06:43.860 --> 00:06:46.770
This is really interesting to find out, because
I feel like it's a little like that thing

00:06:46.770 --> 00:06:49.740
where you're like, "Oh, well my parents will
know the answer and no worries."

00:06:49.740 --> 00:06:52.762
I just assume that there's a set goal and
plan.

00:06:52.762 --> 00:06:57.000
HAASS: One of the things I've learned over
the last few years, I don't assume anything

00:06:57.000 --> 00:06:58.090
anymore.

00:06:58.090 --> 00:07:00.100
About our own country, about the world.

00:07:00.100 --> 00:07:01.100
SIERRA: Fair enough.

00:07:01.100 --> 00:07:05.531
HAASS: And even this war, think of how many
people, even after close to 200,000 Russian

00:07:05.531 --> 00:07:11.610
troops mobilized on Ukraine's border, "assumed"
that countries don't do that anymore.

00:07:11.610 --> 00:07:12.610
SIERRA: Right.

00:07:12.610 --> 00:07:15.710
HAASS: January 6th domestically, how many
of us would've assumed that kind of thing

00:07:15.710 --> 00:07:17.270
can't happen here?

00:07:17.270 --> 00:07:20.699
People would never not accept an election
or try to overturn it.

00:07:20.699 --> 00:07:24.490
So I think what we're learning the hard way
is all bets are off.

00:07:24.490 --> 00:07:29.730
Also, it's one thing in an abstract academic
way at the beginning of a conflict to say,

00:07:29.730 --> 00:07:31.419
"Okay, here's what our war aims are."

00:07:31.419 --> 00:07:35.330
And I've been involved in those drills in
government, but the conflict itself can change

00:07:35.330 --> 00:07:36.330
them.

00:07:36.330 --> 00:07:39.319
Also in this case, the conflict wasn't expected
by most people.

00:07:39.319 --> 00:07:43.110
Remember when the United States was running
around saying, "Hey, these guys aren't just

00:07:43.110 --> 00:07:44.110
mobilizing.

00:07:44.110 --> 00:07:45.110
They're going to invade."

00:07:45.110 --> 00:07:46.110
And people said "No no."

00:07:46.110 --> 00:07:47.590
Including Zelenskyy, including President Zelenskyy.

00:07:47.590 --> 00:07:52.879
And so it's not clear to me when this war
began, we had a set of aims because most people

00:07:52.879 --> 00:07:54.750
didn't think it was going to come about.

00:07:54.750 --> 00:08:00.849
And since it began for good reason, everybody's
been so focused on how to not lose the war.

00:08:00.849 --> 00:08:06.120
Again, that's the questions of arms to provide
intelligence, training, sanctions, what have

00:08:06.120 --> 00:08:07.120
you.

00:08:07.120 --> 00:08:08.550
That's been the understandable focus.

00:08:08.550 --> 00:08:09.550
This sounds like something.

00:08:09.550 --> 00:08:11.070
Well, we'll get to that later.

00:08:11.070 --> 00:08:16.289
So my guess is this is you have your inbox,
your outbox, and I'll get to that later box.

00:08:16.289 --> 00:08:18.470
My hunch is that's in the, I'll get to that
later.

00:08:18.470 --> 00:08:20.581
My point is simply, later is now.

00:08:20.581 --> 00:08:26.870
Now that we're on the cusp of a major battle,
that could prove really significant for this

00:08:26.870 --> 00:08:32.680
war, we have got to think about what is it
we want to bring about and what is it we want

00:08:32.680 --> 00:08:33.680
to avoid?

00:08:33.680 --> 00:08:35.169
What can't we live with?

00:08:35.169 --> 00:08:39.909
And those are questions we had better ask
ourselves and more important start to answer.

00:08:39.909 --> 00:08:45.100
SIERRA: What would it mean for it to end with
Russia gaining territory?

00:08:45.100 --> 00:08:48.170
Does that establish a very dangerous precedent?

00:08:48.170 --> 00:08:51.100
HAASS: My short answer would be yes.

00:08:51.100 --> 00:08:55.730
And that would be, to me, bad for both Ukraine,
because the question is whether Ukraine was

00:08:55.730 --> 00:08:59.791
really viable, if you had Russia in control
of large chunks of the country and you're

00:08:59.791 --> 00:09:04.209
sitting in Kyiv in government, what kind of
autonomy and independence and sovereignty

00:09:04.209 --> 00:09:05.209
do you really have?

00:09:05.209 --> 00:09:08.910
How do you know Russia might not restart a
war in the afternoon?

00:09:08.910 --> 00:09:14.600
But also if Russia were to maintain control
of lots of territory, it's obviously inconsistent

00:09:14.600 --> 00:09:21.040
with the basic international tenant rule that
sovereignty is to be respected.

00:09:21.040 --> 00:09:25.560
Territory is not to be acquired by force to
the extent there's a basis for order in the

00:09:25.560 --> 00:09:26.560
world.

00:09:26.560 --> 00:09:28.610
That's probably the most fundamental principle.

00:09:28.610 --> 00:09:32.790
So the question is, if it's violated here,
what else might Vladimir Putin do?

00:09:32.790 --> 00:09:33.810
What else might others do?

00:09:33.810 --> 00:09:38.610
If they basically said he can get away with
it, how might China or somebody else read

00:09:38.610 --> 00:09:40.660
that and say, "Wow, that's interesting."

00:09:40.660 --> 00:09:42.870
Maybe that's a precedent for me.

00:09:42.870 --> 00:09:47.950
So my problem with Russia succeeding, even
though they've reduced their definition of

00:09:47.950 --> 00:09:50.269
success, apparently, I don't like it.

00:09:50.269 --> 00:09:51.540
I don't like it for Ukraine.

00:09:51.540 --> 00:09:56.410
I don't like it for the precedent it sets,
so I would think a reasonable goal is that

00:09:56.410 --> 00:09:57.410
we push back.

00:09:57.410 --> 00:10:02.110
We give Ukraine the means to push back if
at all possible to get them at least to where

00:10:02.110 --> 00:10:06.019
things stood at the end of February before
this round of the war began.

00:10:06.019 --> 00:10:12.089
Do you think that there's a risk of the US
and Europe just getting tired of this as it

00:10:12.089 --> 00:10:15.240
goes on and sort of pulling back from it?

00:10:15.240 --> 00:10:17.300
HAASS: The answer is yes.

00:10:17.300 --> 00:10:21.450
Might not be uniform, but people will say,
"Well, we've got to focus now on something

00:10:21.450 --> 00:10:22.450
else."

00:10:22.450 --> 00:10:25.970
Some other crisis might come along or they
might just get tired of this.

00:10:25.970 --> 00:10:29.779
They could get tired of things like higher
energy prices.

00:10:29.779 --> 00:10:30.779
That's possible.

00:10:30.779 --> 00:10:33.339
You can also have political changes in certain
countries.

00:10:33.339 --> 00:10:37.370
So you could have different governments, they
could have very different policies and developments

00:10:37.370 --> 00:10:39.519
on the ground will affect policy.

00:10:39.519 --> 00:10:40.639
People will say, "Oh, it's hopeless."

00:10:40.639 --> 00:10:42.600
Or they'll say just the opposite.

00:10:42.600 --> 00:10:43.720
They'll say “it's too expensive.”

00:10:43.720 --> 00:10:45.579
When people start seeing the tab.

00:10:45.579 --> 00:10:51.040
President just announced another $800 million
dollars package of arms going to Ukraine.

00:10:51.040 --> 00:10:56.579
But if this is a long war, which I think is
more likely than not, if there's no peace

00:10:56.579 --> 00:11:02.040
agreement that "terminates the war," we could
be doing 800 million dollars every couple

00:11:02.040 --> 00:11:06.130
of weeks, we could be doing several hundred
million dollars of humanitarian aid every

00:11:06.130 --> 00:11:08.160
couple of weeks, not just us but other countries.

00:11:08.160 --> 00:11:13.240
So this could become an extraordinarily costly,
for all the protagonists, for ourselves, as

00:11:13.240 --> 00:11:15.070
well as for Russia.

00:11:15.070 --> 00:11:16.089
And you could have pushback.

00:11:16.089 --> 00:11:18.422
And that's why, again, this debate over war
aims becomes important.

00:11:18.422 --> 00:11:23.300
Because some people are going to be saying,
it is worth fighting for, I expect there'll

00:11:23.300 --> 00:11:26.360
be those who say, "No, it's not."

00:11:26.360 --> 00:11:30.670
In this country, there's the historic tension
between foreign policy and domestic policy.

00:11:30.670 --> 00:11:32.470
So-called guns versus butter.

00:11:32.470 --> 00:11:35.470
And I would think there'll be those who are
say, "Well, we've done a lot for these people,

00:11:35.470 --> 00:11:36.899
but what about doing more for ourselves?"

00:11:36.899 --> 00:11:40.990
I'm not advocating that, but I would expect
we're going to hear those voices, by the way,

00:11:40.990 --> 00:11:43.089
on both sides of the political divide.

00:11:43.089 --> 00:11:48.470
There'll be conservatives and progressives
alike, who will likely argue that at some

00:11:48.470 --> 00:11:49.470
point.

00:11:49.470 --> 00:11:50.470
SIERRA: Yeah.

00:11:50.470 --> 00:11:53.510
You said that, you know, foreign policy, doesn't
always make it to the top of people's concern

00:11:53.510 --> 00:11:54.510
list.

00:11:54.510 --> 00:11:56.000
HAASS: And even if it does, it doesn't stay
there.

00:11:56.000 --> 00:12:02.910
And again, here we are after two months of
war and if you turn on lots of cable television,

00:12:02.910 --> 00:12:07.339
this is dominating the shows, not a hundred
percent, but dominating it.

00:12:07.339 --> 00:12:10.080
The question is will it still be in two more
months?

00:12:10.080 --> 00:12:11.829
Or in four more months and so forth?

00:12:11.829 --> 00:12:14.600
So I have my doubts.

00:12:14.600 --> 00:12:17.270
And a lot will also, though, depend upon the
nature of the fighting.

00:12:17.270 --> 00:12:20.950
What's also really affected things has been
the atrocities.

00:12:20.950 --> 00:12:25.800
There's been a human quality to this war that
this is not a battlefield war indeed.

00:12:25.800 --> 00:12:30.550
Central to Russian strategy has to make it
anything but a battlefield war, but instead

00:12:30.550 --> 00:12:32.370
to target civilians in cities.

00:12:32.370 --> 00:12:37.620
And so when civilians living in cities elsewhere
in the world see that, they have an identification

00:12:37.620 --> 00:12:38.620
with this.

00:12:38.620 --> 00:12:42.329
This is not in that sense a traditional war
between armies, there's that dimension.

00:12:42.329 --> 00:12:46.670
And we're going to see it intensively in the
south and east in the coming weeks.

00:12:46.670 --> 00:12:52.490
But this is also a war where missiles and
artillery shells are being fired against civilians

00:12:52.490 --> 00:12:54.560
living in towns and cities.

00:12:54.560 --> 00:12:59.700
So when people in Europe in particular see
that, but also in this country, there's an

00:12:59.700 --> 00:13:01.279
understandable, “oh my God” reaction.

00:13:01.279 --> 00:13:02.680
SIERRA: And we are seeing it.

00:13:02.680 --> 00:13:05.649
We're seeing it as it happens on TikTok and
different platforms.

00:13:05.649 --> 00:13:06.649
HAASS: Exactly right.

00:13:06.649 --> 00:13:07.649
Again, exactly right.

00:13:07.649 --> 00:13:08.649
So this is monstrous.

00:13:08.649 --> 00:13:11.670
This is barbaric and people therefore feel
we've got to do things.

00:13:11.670 --> 00:13:15.490
And I actually think that's had a lot to do
with the Western reaction.

00:13:15.490 --> 00:13:19.410
It's interesting if you read the debate in
Germany, the big loophole in the sanctions

00:13:19.410 --> 00:13:24.440
is that the Germans still haven't shut off
their gas imports.

00:13:24.440 --> 00:13:30.329
And the question is these images, and we’re
now seeing these terrible stories out of Mariupol

00:13:30.329 --> 00:13:34.780
about these large trenches with hundreds or
even thousands of innocent people killed and

00:13:34.780 --> 00:13:40.671
put in them, is whether this affects the calculus
in Germany and people say, "Hey, I know it

00:13:40.671 --> 00:13:44.990
would be incredibly costly to our economy
if we were to shut off or dramatically reduce

00:13:44.990 --> 00:13:49.300
imports of Russian gas, but we have to because
we're financing this war."

00:13:49.300 --> 00:13:51.360
And it'll be very interesting to watch that
debate.

00:13:51.360 --> 00:14:00.010
SIERRA: Well thinking of natural gas and oil
and energy, it feels like the west is sanctioning

00:14:00.010 --> 00:14:06.880
Russia but also still buying its energy, doesn't
that play against itself?

00:14:06.880 --> 00:14:09.130
HAASS: The short answer is yes.

00:14:09.130 --> 00:14:12.919
Russia's main export is energy, coal, oil,
gas.

00:14:12.919 --> 00:14:15.259
Coal and oil are essentially global commodities.

00:14:15.259 --> 00:14:18.990
Because you put them on a tanker, you put
them on a barge and you can send them anywhere.

00:14:18.990 --> 00:14:21.089
You can't send them to Europe, you can send
them somewhere else.

00:14:21.089 --> 00:14:22.579
And that's what's happening.

00:14:22.579 --> 00:14:27.579
The sanctions against Russia are not universally
supported, indeed, just the opposite.

00:14:27.579 --> 00:14:33.040
A clear majority, an overwhelming majority
of the world's countries, are not supporting

00:14:33.040 --> 00:14:34.040
sanctions.

00:14:34.040 --> 00:14:38.360
I don't know if it's two thirds or three quarters
or four fifths, but out of 190 or so countries

00:14:38.360 --> 00:14:43.440
in the United Nations General Assembly, I'd
be surprised if more than 50 or so, were supporting

00:14:43.440 --> 00:14:44.440
sanctions.

00:14:44.440 --> 00:14:48.389
So Russia is still able to offload its oil.

00:14:48.389 --> 00:14:52.730
Russia's still offloading its coal and oil,
even though we're not importing any energy

00:14:52.730 --> 00:14:53.730
from Russia.

00:14:53.730 --> 00:14:57.480
And even though the Europeans have announced,
they're no longer accepting coal, I think

00:14:57.480 --> 00:14:59.970
they're in the process of no longer accepting
oil.

00:14:59.970 --> 00:15:01.149
Gas though is different.

00:15:01.149 --> 00:15:03.149
Gas is not easily moved around.

00:15:03.149 --> 00:15:08.209
Either you have to have pipelines to ship
it or you have to liquefy it, put it on boats

00:15:08.209 --> 00:15:10.260
and then un-liquify it.

00:15:10.260 --> 00:15:14.529
That is easier said than, and to build up
the capacity to do the latter, either one

00:15:14.529 --> 00:15:15.529
can take years.

00:15:15.529 --> 00:15:20.089
Either you have to build new pipelines or
you've got to build ways of liquefying and

00:15:20.089 --> 00:15:22.089
both exporting and importing the gas.

00:15:22.089 --> 00:15:26.990
HAASS: So that's the one commodity Russia
exports that makes hundreds of millions of

00:15:26.990 --> 00:15:33.130
dollars a day from, that Europe can't find,
in near term, a substitute for.

00:15:33.130 --> 00:15:36.100
And at the moment have not been willing to
go without.

00:15:36.100 --> 00:15:37.830
But we are financing this war.

00:15:37.830 --> 00:15:42.430
Russia is making well over $500 million a
day.

00:15:42.430 --> 00:15:47.350
So whatever the costs of the war, whatever
the costs of the sanctions, Russia's doing

00:15:47.350 --> 00:15:48.350
okay.

00:15:48.350 --> 00:15:52.160
It can sustain a long war in particular because
the price of energy, even though it's come

00:15:52.160 --> 00:15:56.529
down a little bit in the last couple of days,
has gone up dramatically over the course of

00:15:56.529 --> 00:15:57.600
the last year.

00:15:57.600 --> 00:16:02.420
So Russia can actually export less energy,
but still get as much or even more money for

00:16:02.420 --> 00:16:05.620
it, given that the price is going up.

00:16:05.620 --> 00:16:08.810
SIERRA: To go back to something dark that
you mentioned, you know, there are a lot of

00:16:08.810 --> 00:16:10.220
atrocities going on.

00:16:10.220 --> 00:16:16.079
So there's been a lot of coverage on war crimes
and I'm still trying to figure it out.

00:16:16.079 --> 00:16:21.220
So Russia seems to have like no guardrails
in this war and the attacks against civilians

00:16:21.220 --> 00:16:24.680
have been, as you said, brutal and ruthless.

00:16:24.680 --> 00:16:28.590
But war is brutal and ruthless, right?

00:16:28.590 --> 00:16:33.420
So where does the distinction lie between
war and war crimes?

00:16:33.420 --> 00:16:35.710
And also when is the time to talk about this?

00:16:35.710 --> 00:16:36.710
HAASS: Okay.

00:16:36.710 --> 00:16:37.710
Two good questions.

00:16:37.710 --> 00:16:40.870
There are rules and codes and laws of war.

00:16:40.870 --> 00:16:44.569
What we're seeing now is a violation of every
one of them.

00:16:44.569 --> 00:16:51.339
War is meant to be fought between combatants,
people in uniform, on battlefield, with proportionality

00:16:51.339 --> 00:16:52.339
and so forth.

00:16:52.339 --> 00:16:56.029
Lots of traditions, religious and legal traditions,
to do this.

00:16:56.029 --> 00:16:58.520
You are not supposed to target non-combatants.

00:16:58.520 --> 00:17:02.930
Now it's one thing if it happens accidentally,
this is not accidentally.

00:17:02.930 --> 00:17:05.470
Accidents don't happen six times a day.

00:17:05.470 --> 00:17:07.380
Accidents may happen once or twice.

00:17:07.380 --> 00:17:09.700
This is the purposeful, intentional targeting.

00:17:09.700 --> 00:17:11.699
So this is a war crime.

00:17:11.699 --> 00:17:18.190
And what we're also seeing is unlawful treatment
of people who are taken, these mass executions.

00:17:18.190 --> 00:17:23.110
This is happening with a regularity and on
a scale, this is not an improvisation.

00:17:23.110 --> 00:17:24.339
This is clearly ordered.

00:17:24.339 --> 00:17:25.670
So let's just say that.

00:17:25.670 --> 00:17:28.199
The question then is what do you do about
it?

00:17:28.199 --> 00:17:32.740
And I would say, I don't know any way of doing
something about it now.

00:17:32.740 --> 00:17:37.270
And I hope that doesn't sound bad or callous
because again, it's horrific, these images,

00:17:37.270 --> 00:17:39.950
it's hard to sleep after you see them.

00:17:39.950 --> 00:17:41.910
The problem is the Russians are doing it.

00:17:41.910 --> 00:17:43.690
It's central to their strategy.

00:17:43.690 --> 00:17:48.300
So until we can stop the war, I don't know
how we stop this.

00:17:48.300 --> 00:17:52.789
Now the day will come and it should come where
there's accountability and that's why there's

00:17:52.789 --> 00:17:54.700
War Crimes Tribunals, and the rest.

00:17:54.700 --> 00:17:58.990
And you gather the evidence, and ultimately
there's international court of law, but that

00:17:58.990 --> 00:18:01.750
always comes afterwards, often years after
this.

00:18:01.750 --> 00:18:06.750
You[c] can talk about it now and you can document
it now, both to prepare for that day.

00:18:06.750 --> 00:18:12.400
Also, hopefully it will discourage or deter
some individuals on the Russian side from

00:18:12.400 --> 00:18:17.480
participating in these activities because
they will be held accountable.

00:18:17.480 --> 00:18:19.130
But I don't see a lot of evidence of that.

00:18:19.130 --> 00:18:22.000
They're going to say they were ordered or
they're more frightened about not following

00:18:22.000 --> 00:18:26.330
the orders than they are about being held
accountable down the road.

00:18:26.330 --> 00:18:28.620
So I don't have any answers for you.

00:18:28.620 --> 00:18:32.830
And I think the best thing that can be done
is to fight the wars effectively is it can

00:18:32.830 --> 00:18:35.890
be fought, to end the wars as soon as it can
be ended.

00:18:35.890 --> 00:18:41.049
And then we can turn to this, but I don't
see any way to effectively address this now.

00:18:41.049 --> 00:18:43.660
SIERRA: Even though it's being recorded, even
though it's being shared?

00:18:43.660 --> 00:18:46.730
HAASS: Even though it's being recorded, even
though it's being shared, even though it is

00:18:46.730 --> 00:18:48.770
as horrific as it is.

00:18:48.770 --> 00:18:52.010
Again, it's not incidental to the Russian
style of war.

00:18:52.010 --> 00:18:55.039
In some ways it is the Russian style of war.

00:18:55.039 --> 00:18:56.039
SIERRA: Ok.

00:18:56.039 --> 00:19:00.820
Well then on the other hand let’s talk about
the Ukrainian style of war.

00:19:00.820 --> 00:19:04.300
They seem to be doing well - better than anyone
expected.

00:19:04.300 --> 00:19:11.090
And, with all respect to their bravery, I
want to ask you, why is Ukraine doing so well?

00:19:11.090 --> 00:19:16.600
HAASS: Well, some of the reasons are accrued
to Ukraine itself, the leadership, the spirit,

00:19:16.600 --> 00:19:18.130
the motivation.

00:19:18.130 --> 00:19:22.190
Armies are not just about what's called orders
of battle, the number of tanks and Howitzers

00:19:22.190 --> 00:19:23.260
and planes and the rest.

00:19:23.260 --> 00:19:28.840
It's also about the commitment of the troops,
the cause, the quality of leadership.

00:19:28.840 --> 00:19:31.350
And I think there, they have clear advantages
over the Russians.

00:19:31.350 --> 00:19:37.370
They're obviously getting enormous material
help from the United States and NATO and other

00:19:37.370 --> 00:19:38.370
countries.

00:19:38.370 --> 00:19:39.840
We've also helped them with two other things.

00:19:39.840 --> 00:19:43.730
One is intelligence, they are getting regular
inputs of intelligence.

00:19:43.730 --> 00:19:48.690
I don't think all these Russian Generals have
simply met their end coincidentally, I think

00:19:48.690 --> 00:19:51.870
there's been some pretty good intelligence
support with that.

00:19:51.870 --> 00:19:52.870
And then also training.

00:19:52.870 --> 00:19:58.850
It wasn't talked about a whole lot, but the
2014 experience when Russia took Crimea and

00:19:58.850 --> 00:20:03.740
entered the Donbas, it showed the disparity
between Russia and Ukraine and Ukrainians

00:20:03.740 --> 00:20:05.700
knew they had a real problem on their hands.

00:20:05.700 --> 00:20:10.460
And quietly, but consistently over these eight
years, there's been a large training effort,

00:20:10.460 --> 00:20:15.350
largely from the United States, also some
others to really help Ukraine improve the

00:20:15.350 --> 00:20:20.380
quality of its armed forces, changed a lot
of the management and leadership structures,

00:20:20.380 --> 00:20:22.770
almost the culture, the way I'd put it.

00:20:22.770 --> 00:20:28.880
And in some ways became less Soviet, less
top heavy, less anti-initiative and much more

00:20:28.880 --> 00:20:32.289
adaptive and flexible, a little bit more like
the American model.

00:20:32.289 --> 00:20:35.550
And I think we're seeing some of the dividends
of having done that.

00:20:35.550 --> 00:20:39.500
SIERRA: I have a question that I don't think
we touched on last time and I've been hearing

00:20:39.500 --> 00:20:43.701
less and less about it, but it was something
that we all were taught to be scared of, which

00:20:43.701 --> 00:20:45.150
is Russian hacking.

00:20:45.150 --> 00:20:51.760
And I've heard they were really good at hacking,
but it doesn't seem like we've seen major

00:20:51.760 --> 00:20:52.770
cyber attacks.

00:20:52.770 --> 00:20:55.059
So is it that this just isn't a concern anymore?

00:20:55.059 --> 00:20:58.260
Or is it that they weren't as good as we thought?

00:20:58.260 --> 00:20:59.520
HAASS: It's curious.

00:20:59.520 --> 00:21:02.890
The old Sherlock Holmes line about it's the
dog that didn't bark in the night.

00:21:02.890 --> 00:21:07.130
This is what hasn't happened that so many
people including me expected.

00:21:07.130 --> 00:21:10.960
And traditionally, if you were going to put
on a piece of paper, Russia's comparative

00:21:10.960 --> 00:21:13.650
advantages, we would've put cyber.

00:21:13.650 --> 00:21:16.910
Mr. Putin does not seem to be the kind of
guy who holds back.

00:21:16.910 --> 00:21:18.940
So why don't we see a lot out of cyber?

00:21:18.940 --> 00:21:22.920
So either they're not as good as we thought,
we're better protected than we thought, or

00:21:22.920 --> 00:21:25.420
he's worried about our ability to retaliate.

00:21:25.420 --> 00:21:29.790
I don't know the answer to that, but I am
struck by what hasn't, or at least appears

00:21:29.790 --> 00:21:30.860
to have happened there.

00:21:30.860 --> 00:21:36.850
You also read lots of stories about how Ukraine
has been really good at organizing with international

00:21:36.850 --> 00:21:39.980
as well as domestic support there, almost
a counter hacking.

00:21:39.980 --> 00:21:40.980
SIERRA: Right.

00:21:40.980 --> 00:21:41.980
Yeah.

00:21:41.980 --> 00:21:44.050
HAASS: Which has been helping their defenses,
but also going, so I don't know if the Russians

00:21:44.050 --> 00:21:48.590
have been simply pushed on the back of their
feet and off balance or some combination of

00:21:48.590 --> 00:21:50.100
all these things, but you're right.

00:21:50.100 --> 00:21:54.260
If you had asked me, would we be two months
into the war and we would not have seen massive

00:21:54.260 --> 00:21:55.360
cyber attacks?

00:21:55.360 --> 00:21:57.450
I would've said no, we would've seen them.

00:21:57.450 --> 00:21:59.240
And so it's striking by its absence.

00:21:59.240 --> 00:22:03.710
SIERRA: In line with that, it seems like a
lot of coverage has just been expressing surprise

00:22:03.710 --> 00:22:06.080
that Russia isn't doing better.

00:22:06.080 --> 00:22:10.490
So does that sort of mean that Russia is weaker
than we kind of thought.

00:22:10.490 --> 00:22:12.660
HAASS: I think the answer is yes.

00:22:12.660 --> 00:22:16.670
And again, that'll be an interesting thing
afterwards, which is why did both Western

00:22:16.670 --> 00:22:23.070
intelligence and Vladimir Putin both overestimate
the strength of the Russian military?

00:22:23.070 --> 00:22:26.200
And he was getting fed presumably bad information.

00:22:26.200 --> 00:22:29.880
Obviously, there's more corruption, a lot
of the money wasn't going to certain things.

00:22:29.880 --> 00:22:31.430
A lot of things don't seem to work.

00:22:31.430 --> 00:22:38.059
The failure rate appears to be extraordinarily
high, the quality or lack of it, of military

00:22:38.059 --> 00:22:43.440
leadership, the Russians are also, they seem
to be, if you think about Russian history,

00:22:43.440 --> 00:22:46.200
much better at resisting invasion than invading.

00:22:46.200 --> 00:22:47.850
They're not good at improvising.

00:22:47.850 --> 00:22:49.080
They get very locked into…

00:22:49.080 --> 00:22:53.270
And again, this is not a culture politically
and otherwise we're improvisation, shall we

00:22:53.270 --> 00:22:54.799
say is rewarded.

00:22:54.799 --> 00:22:59.730
So for a lot of different reasons, they're
doing worse than pretty much anybody predicted.

00:22:59.730 --> 00:23:05.180
So I think we need to go back and basically
ask, why did we get that wrong?

00:23:05.180 --> 00:23:06.760
And what does this tell us about the future?

00:23:06.760 --> 00:23:10.429
Because I think it's part of the explanation
of why Russia after this war, whenever that

00:23:10.429 --> 00:23:15.210
day comes, or if this war continues against
the backdrop of this war is going to be weaker,

00:23:15.210 --> 00:23:17.299
then we thought.

00:23:17.299 --> 00:23:20.710
I'd love to know, and I don't know the answer
to this, what the Chinese make of it.

00:23:20.710 --> 00:23:24.510
About what the Chinese not just make of the
Russians, but whether this has forced them

00:23:24.510 --> 00:23:26.270
in some ways to reexamine themselves.

00:23:26.270 --> 00:23:27.270
SIERRA: Oh really?

00:23:27.270 --> 00:23:29.280
HAASS: What are their assumptions about their
abilities?

00:23:29.280 --> 00:23:30.280
I mean, think about it.

00:23:30.280 --> 00:23:32.540
What's the biggest strategic goal of China?

00:23:32.540 --> 00:23:33.540
Taiwan.

00:23:33.540 --> 00:23:34.540
What hasn't China ever done?

00:23:34.540 --> 00:23:36.290
A large amphibious operation.

00:23:36.290 --> 00:23:40.640
So what do they assume about their abilities
to fight a war?

00:23:40.640 --> 00:23:45.670
How confident are they of their own estimates
of their own capabilities?

00:23:45.670 --> 00:23:51.351
And so this has taught us again to, to be
somewhat skeptical of assumptions and to ask

00:23:51.351 --> 00:23:52.351
questions.

00:23:52.351 --> 00:23:56.730
And I'm hoping the US intelligence community,
when all is done, does an after action report

00:23:56.730 --> 00:24:00.230
or a so-called postmortem and looks at it
and says, "Okay.

00:24:00.230 --> 00:24:01.230
Where were we right?

00:24:01.230 --> 00:24:02.539
Where were we wrong and why?

00:24:02.539 --> 00:24:07.090
What does that tell us about things like confirmation
bias, about assumptions?

00:24:07.090 --> 00:24:11.210
Is it that we didn't have the right information
or is it that we interpreted the information

00:24:11.210 --> 00:24:12.680
we had incorrectly?"

00:24:12.680 --> 00:24:14.330
I'll be really interested in that.

00:24:14.330 --> 00:24:21.240
SIERRA: All right Richard Haass, we’re coming
to the end, so I have to ask why does the

00:24:21.240 --> 00:24:23.860
war in Ukraine still matter?

00:24:23.860 --> 00:24:26.130
HAASS: It should matter for several reasons.

00:24:26.130 --> 00:24:27.750
One is the humanitarian.

00:24:27.750 --> 00:24:31.460
It's hard to watch this and not have an answer
to that question.

00:24:31.460 --> 00:24:37.000
It may not affect people directly in terms
of affecting our lives, our work, our families,

00:24:37.000 --> 00:24:42.470
but as a human being, it's hard to watch this
and not be moved or outraged or some combination

00:24:42.470 --> 00:24:43.470
of all of that.

00:24:43.470 --> 00:24:47.840
Second of all, when president Zelenskyy says,
"We're fighting for all of you," he's right.

00:24:47.840 --> 00:24:52.610
If Russia isn't stopped in Ukraine, does anyone
think that Mr. Putin is the kind of guy who

00:24:52.610 --> 00:24:54.279
self-stops?

00:24:54.279 --> 00:24:55.360
I don't think so.

00:24:55.360 --> 00:24:58.730
I think he pushes and where he finds weakness,
he continues to push.

00:24:58.730 --> 00:25:00.039
Where there's pushback, he stops.

00:25:00.039 --> 00:25:06.580
In the Ukraine, for example, he's again, revised
his goals down because he met with extraordinary

00:25:06.580 --> 00:25:07.580
resistance.

00:25:07.580 --> 00:25:11.090
But why does anyone think for a second he
wouldn't revise them up?

00:25:11.090 --> 00:25:16.100
And the indirect thing, when the North Koreans,
the Chinese, the Iranians terrorist groups

00:25:16.100 --> 00:25:17.980
and all that, they look at this and they go,
"Okay.

00:25:17.980 --> 00:25:19.490
What does this tell us?"

00:25:19.490 --> 00:25:24.020
And if we don't stand up here, why do people
think they won't be more likely to challenge

00:25:24.020 --> 00:25:25.020
us?

00:25:25.020 --> 00:25:27.740
And then there's things like energy, there's
connections, supply chains.

00:25:27.740 --> 00:25:30.590
So this has all sorts of economic consequences.

00:25:30.590 --> 00:25:34.919
I think consequences for war and peace, obviously
human consequences.

00:25:34.919 --> 00:25:40.290
So I think this is a good example, this may
be 4,000 miles away, whatever the distance

00:25:40.290 --> 00:25:46.390
is, 5,000 miles away, but it's a good example
that things can, and do matter.

00:25:46.390 --> 00:25:51.149
Two months ago, Gabby, very few of us could
have looked and either found Ukraine on the

00:25:51.149 --> 00:25:54.010
map, or even if we found it could have said,
"This is Odessa.

00:25:54.010 --> 00:25:55.460
This is Mariupol.

00:25:55.460 --> 00:25:56.460
This is Lviv.

00:25:56.460 --> 00:25:57.460
This is Kyiv."

00:25:57.460 --> 00:26:00.340
We didn't even pronounce it Kyiv several months
ago.

00:26:00.340 --> 00:26:05.010
And I think what we're learning is that this
country in the center of Europe, the cities

00:26:05.010 --> 00:26:11.120
in that country, we are now beginning to see
the associations between what happens there

00:26:11.120 --> 00:26:13.860
and what happens elsewhere in the world and
including here.

00:26:13.860 --> 00:26:15.500
So yeah, that's why it matters.

00:26:15.500 --> 00:26:19.100
SIERRA: Well, this has been great and you
know always good hanging with you.

00:26:19.100 --> 00:26:24.140
HAASS: I look forward to coming on your show
one day and where we can leave people happier

